---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy#howto',
  name: 'The DJ USB Backup Strategy: Two Drives, Zero Panic',
  description: 'The exact backup system professional DJs use: two identical drives, mirrored exports, physical separation. One evening to set up, a career without USB panic.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/dj-usb-backup-strategy.png',
  // THE STEPS ARE NOW THE PAGE'S OWN WORDS. Rewritten 2026-08-04.
  //
  // The three steps here used to be a summary nobody could find on the page:
  // "Two quality 32-64GB drives, same brand and model, formatted FAT32 + MBR"
  // is not a sentence this article contains. Google's structured data policy
  // requires HowTo steps to be visible on the page, the same rule that got
  // the site's mismatched FAQPage blocks deleted. These four are the four
  // items under "The setup (one evening)", word for word, minus markup.
  //
  // Only the SETUP list is marked up. "The habit (per gig)" below it is a
  // recurring routine, not steps of this procedure, so it stays out rather
  // than being folded in to make the list look longer.
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Buy two identical drives',
      text: 'Buy two identical drives, same brand, same model, same size. Identical drives behave identically on players.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Format both',
      text: 'Format both: FAT32 + MBR.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Export to both, back to back',
      text: 'Export to both, back to back, in rekordbox EXPORT mode. Same playlists, same order. Eject each with the rekordbox button.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Label them',
      text: 'Label them. A and B, tape or marker. In a dark booth you want zero thinking.',
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base
  title="The DJ USB Backup Strategy: Two Drives | Save My Gig"
  description="Two identical drives, the same export on both, kept apart: the backup that works while you are standing in the booth. One evening to set up."
  path="/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy"
  crumbName="Backup strategy"
  ogImage="/og/dj-usb-backup-strategy.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">Backup_Strategy</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">TWO DRIVES,<br><span class="accent">ZERO PANIC</span></h1>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>The whole system in one sentence:</strong> two identical USB drives, the same export on both, kept in two different pockets. Everything else on this site exists for people who don't do this yet. And <a href="/recovery">if a drive already died on you</a>, start there and come back to this page after.</p>
    </div>

        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Why redundancy beats recovery</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>Every recovery technique, ours included, is a gamble played under pressure. A mirrored second drive is not a gamble: it's a fifteen-second swap while the room keeps dancing. The pros you never hear panic stories about aren't lucky. They carry the boring second drive.</p>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The setup (one evening)</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Buy two identical drives</strong>, same brand, same model, same size. Identical drives behave identically on players. <a href="/gear">The models we trust →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Format both:</strong> <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32</span> + <span class="mono" translate="no">MBR</span>. <a href="/fix/format-usb-for-cdj">Step-by-step →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Export to both, back to back,</strong> in rekordbox EXPORT mode. Same playlists, same order. Eject each with the rekordbox button.</li>
      <li><strong>Label them.</strong> A and B, tape or marker. In a dark booth you want zero thinking.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The habit (per gig)</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Library changed this week? Re-export to <strong>both</strong> drives. An outdated backup is a trap.</li>
      <li>Drive A in the gig bag. Drive B on your keychain or jacket. <strong>Never the same pocket, never the same bag.</strong></li>
      <li>At the booth: play from A. If A misbehaves mid-set, eject, insert B, keep mixing. That's the whole emergency plan.</li>
      <li>Once a month: back up the rekordbox library itself (File → Library → Backup Library) to a third place, cloud or home drive.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">What a second drive actually removes</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>Two drives are not two independent chances. You export to both from the same laptop, back to back, from the same library, so the causes that arrive through the export arrive on both: a bad export, a withdrawn rekordbox version, two sticks from the same production batch. Claiming the odds multiply into nothing would be wrong.</p>
    <p>What the second drive does remove is the failure that actually ends gigs: one physical drive dying, or being dropped, lost or corrupted, in one booth, with the room waiting. That failure is common, it is the one you cannot fix on site, and the answer to it is a fifteen-second swap. You cannot buy that in one perfect drive at any price; you can have it for the cost of a second cheap one.</p>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="backup_strategy" />
  </main>
</Base>
